Z||000000||1FST2M6||04-05-2000||08:41 AM||||The shop i deal with is offering an awsome deal! just checked their page then called Ed. here it is...  HalTech air/fuel meter and injector duty cycle monitor for  $100... it will wire right into our cars with NO really difficult mods.. simple soldering or wire crimp connectors and about an hour you'll know how hard your injectors are working ad weather or not your car is running right...  I'm getting one... getting ride of my Autometer unit... check them out... <A HREF="http://www.engineered.net" TARGET=_blank>www.engineered.net</A>    OH they also have a HalTECH unit for Twin O2 sensors to read each bank of cylinders instead of a cumuliative total of the two... for even MORE accuracy.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||BBBAD GT||04-05-2000||09:58 AM||||I'll have to say any type of O2 monitor that hooks up to your existing O2's is not the most accurate thing in the world.  The only way you are going to get a accurate reading of your A/F ratio is to hook it up to a wide ban O2 sensor, which run approx 1k (most dyno shops should have these, if they tune forced induction motors).  The only time you need to see your A/F ratio is at WOT, the computer uses the stock O2 sensor to try and get to a theortical 14.7 A/F by constantly switching back and forth from rich to lean.  At least this is what the Ford electronics does.  I have a device that reads the two O2 sensors in the Mustang by volts (0v lean, 1v rich), and it also show duty cycle and pulse width of the injectors.  The duty cycle is pretty handy when you upgrade to bigger injectors and are not sure if they are adequate.  I found out my old 30# injectors were going 100% duty cycle at only 4600 rpms, at 10lb of boost.  I fixed that with 36# injectors and now they are going to 100% duty cycle at an acceptable 5400 rpms. <P>What I'm saying is if you want ball park readings, buy a O2 monitor that hooks to your existing O2.  If you want an accurate reading, go to a shop that has a dyno and wide ban O2 sensor and there won't be any guessing.<P>I just checked out that site, and those products look like the efi systems offerings.  Thats basically what I have, but with digital readouts, not just blinking lights.<P><p>[This message has been edited by BBBAD GT (edited 04-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||04-05-2000||01:28 PM||||the shop bought in to EFI.. now a distributor. and installer. The Wideband you speak of is also available at the shop... available for purchase or for individual tuning. (although they never sold a complete setup to anyone other than Mustang owners) most everyone (around here) gets a PMS and has the shop run it on the dyno to make sure everyhing is working properly.. Under nitrous my injectors are only runnig about 70% @ 5600... And thats how the GM computer works also... tosses the computer between way to rich and about to shut off lean. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||BBBAD GT||04-05-2000||02:10 PM||||Does EFI systems make a PMS for the GM, and more specificall a Fiero computer?  If you want no brainer tuning, this is the setup to go with a poweradder.  Its not the most accurate way to tune (it intercepts the data, and changes it to the computer based on your input), but it is simple to understand.  I have a little rundown of the unit on my web page  <A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/JeffHTML4.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/JeffHTML4.htm</A>  for Fords.<P>BTW, you keeping that Powerdyne Travis, or what?<p>[This message has been edited by BBBAD GT (edited 04-05-2000).]||||reg||1
